<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="031" project="spaarke-notification-spine-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>Extract session-agnostic Layer-A action seam behind CreateNotification/CreateTask/UpdateRecord node executors</title>
    <phase>3 Layer A + Notification flip</phase>
    <gate>blocked-until:030</gate>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>High-blast-radius brownfield extraction directly behind three shipped, chat/playbook-dispatch-critical node executors; touches `.cs` in `Services/Ai/`; 6+ steps; deps on prior task.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>High-blast-radius brownfield extraction behind shipped executors — regressing chat/playbook dispatch is R3 in the plan's risk register (High probability×High impact).</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <effort-reason>Brownfield root-cause work on NodeExecutionContext coupling: the extraction must unwind the coupling without changing two of the three executors' public constructor signatures (existing unit tests depend on them), which requires genuine design judgment, not a mechanical move.</effort-reason>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <deps>030</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, api, refactoring, ai</tags>
    <estimated-effort>6-8h</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Extract a session-agnostic Layer-A action seam (Create Notification / Create Task / Update Record) BEHIND the three existing node executors, exposed via the `PublicContracts` facade (ADR-013), so that later phases (comms-RI Phase 4, suggestions Phase 5) can create these records WITHOUT a chat session or playbook run — while leaving today's chat/playbook dispatch behavior byte-for-byte unchanged (proven by task 030's characterization tests passing unmodified).</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Follow ADRs strictly. Expert in brownfield C# extraction, ADR-013 facade design, and preserving binary/behavioral compatibility of DI-constructed services under existing unit-test coverage.</role>

  <goal>A new session-agnostic seam exists in `Services/Ai/PublicContracts/` (interface + implementation) exposing `CreateNotificationAsync`, `CreateTaskAsync`, and `UpdateRecordAsync` methods that accept plain typed request records (no `NodeExecutionContext`, no chat session, no playbook run) and perform the SAME Dataverse entity creation/update the three node executors perform today. The three executors are refactored to call shared extracted logic internally so their own behavior is unchanged. Task 030's characterization tests pass with ZERO modifications. `CreateTaskNodeExecutor` and `UpdateRecordNodeExecutor`'s existing unit tests (`CreateTaskNodeExecutorTests.cs`, `UpdateRecordNodeExecutorTests.cs`) pass with ZERO modifications, including their exact constructor call signatures. The new seam is independently invokable and covered by a new `tests/integration/seam/**` test proving it works without any chat/playbook infrastructure.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Today, `CreateNotificationNodeExecutor`, `CreateTaskNodeExecutor`, and `UpdateRecordNodeExecutor` are the ONLY code paths that can create an `appnotification`/`task` record or PATCH a Dataverse record from AI-driven logic — and all three require a full `NodeExecutionContext` (playbook run, node config, previous-node outputs, tenant/user context) to invoke. FR-11 (comms-RI, Phase 4), FR-13 (comms-RI actions), and FR-15 (suggestion producer, Phase 5) all need to create these SAME kinds of records from a fire-and-forget enrichment step or a Daily-Briefing render — neither of which has a playbook run or a chat session. Building a second, parallel record-creation path for those producers would violate CLAUDE.md §11 (component justification — extend, don't fork) and would drift from the executors' behavior over time. The correct move is to extract the actual Dataverse entity-building/creation logic that lives INSIDE the three executors into a session-agnostic core the executors themselves call, then expose that core as a facade under `PublicContracts` per ADR-013 (Layer-A seam consumed via the facade; no direct injection of AI internals into non-AI code).

      CRITICAL CONSTRAINT (verified against existing tests 2026-07-20): `tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/Services/Ai/Nodes/CreateTaskNodeExecutorTests.cs` constructs `new CreateTaskNodeExecutor(_templateEngineMock.Object, _entityServiceMock.Object, _loggerMock.Object)` — a 3-argument constructor call. `UpdateRecordNodeExecutorTests.cs` constructs `UpdateRecordNodeExecutor` with its current 4-argument constructor. Per the acceptance criteria (FR-07: "existing executor tests pass unmodified"), these constructor signatures MUST NOT change — adding a new constructor parameter (even with a default) breaks the letter of "unmodified" and risks a compile-time or DI-resolution regression. `CreateNotificationNodeExecutor` has NO existing unit test file (`CreateNotificationNodeExecutorTests.cs` does not exist anywhere under `tests/` as of 2026-07-20) — its constructor has more freedom, but for design consistency across all three executors, treat all three constructors as frozen for this task.

      The correct extraction shape, therefore, is: pull the pure Dataverse entity-building + creation logic (currently private/static methods inside each executor, e.g. `BuildNotificationEntity`, `CheckForDuplicateNotificationAsync`, the inline `task` Entity construction in `CreateTaskNodeExecutor.ExecuteAsync`, and `CoerceFieldValue`/`CoerceStringMapping`/`CoerceChoiceFromMetadata`/`ResolveEntityMetadataAsync` in `UpdateRecordNodeExecutor`) into new session-agnostic core classes that take EXPLICIT typed parameters (title/body/recipientId/... — not `NodeExecutionContext`) plus the Dataverse-boundary service (`IGenericEntityService`/`IFieldMappingDataverseService`) passed in. Both (a) the existing executor — using its own already-injected Dataverse service field — and (b) the new `PublicContracts` seam — using its own separately-injected Dataverse service — call the SAME extracted core. Executor constructors, `Validate`, and public `ExecuteAsync` signatures do not change; only their PRIVATE internals are refactored to delegate to the shared core instead of duplicating the entity-building logic inline.

      Two fields currently baked into `CreateNotificationNodeExecutor.BuildNotificationEntity` are playbook-specific and must become explicit parameters on the extracted core so non-playbook callers (Phase 4/5) can supply their own values: `entity["sprk_source"] = "playbook"` and `entity["sprk_playbookrunid"] = context.RunId.ToString()`. The executor's call site passes `source: "playbook"`, `correlationId: context.RunId.ToString()` to preserve today's exact appnotification field values (characterization-test parity); a future Phase 4/5 caller passes its own source string.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Ai/Nodes/CreateNotificationNodeExecutor.cs</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Ai/Nodes/CreateTaskNodeExecutor.cs</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Ai/Nodes/UpdateRecordNodeExecutor.cs</file>
